postgres — The Server / System User
postgres is not a command in the traditional sense. It refers to two things depending on context:
- The PostgreSQL system user 
When PostgreSQL is installed on Linux, it creates a dedicated OS user called postgres. This user owns the database files and the service.
* sudo -u postgres #run the following command as the postgres user
* sudo -u postgres psql # psql → launch the PostgreSQL client
This is the standard way to get into PostgreSQL on a fresh install, because by default only the postgres OS user can authenticate as the postgres DB superuser via peer authentication.
 
- The postgres binary (the server itself)
Located at something like /usr/lib/postgresql/16/bin/postgres, this is the actual database server daemon.
* postgres -D /var/lib/postgresql/data # -D → specifies the data directory (where the cluster lives)
You rarely run this manually; systemd/pg_ctl usually starts it for you.

psql — The Interactive Client
psql is the PostgreSQL command-line client. It's what you use to type SQL, run scripts, and inspect the database.

Basic syntax
psql [OPTIONS] [DBNAME [USERNAME]]
Common flags
Flag     	Long form    	Meaning
-h    	--host	       Hostname of the server (default: local socket) * psql -h db.example.com -p 5432 -U alice -d appdb #Connect to a specific database on a remote host
-p    	--port	       Port (default: 5432)
-U    	--username   	Connect as this DB user * sudo -u postgres psql #Local connection as the postgres superuser
-d    	--dbname	     Database to connect to
-W    	--password	   Force password prompt
-w	   --no-password	 Never prompt for password
-c	   --command	     Run a single SQL command and exit * psql -U alice -d appdb -c "SELECT version(); #Run a one-off query and exit
-f	   --file	        Run SQL from a file * psql -U alice -d appdb -f migrate.sql #Run a SQL script
-l   	--list        	List all databases and exit * psql -U alice -l  #List databases non-interactively
-t	   --tuples-only	 Print rows only, no headers/footers
-A	   --no-align	    Unaligned output (great for scripting)
-q	  --quiet	        Suppress welcome messages
-v	  --set	          Set a psql variable (e.g. -v ON_ERROR_STOP=1)
-X  	--no-psqlrc	    Ignore ~/.psqlrc
-e	 --echo-queries	  Echo commands sent to the server

--Common "instances" you'll actually see
sudo -u postgres psql	 #Switch to the postgres OS user and open psql
psql -U postgres  #Open psql connecting as the postgres DB user
psql postgres 	#Open psql, connecting to the database named postgres
psql -h localhost -U postgres -d postgres	 #Fully explicit connection
pg_ctl -D /var/lib/postgresql/data start	 #Start the server (uses the postgres binary under the hood)



